\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces An exact address model for $N=3$. Every displayed exponent belongs to one residue lane. The drawing records labels rather than coefficient magnitudes.}}{4}{figure.caption.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The exact signed shift $J_4$. Source columns and target rows describe the same action as the cycle on the left. Direct integer multiplication verifies $J_4^4=-I$.}}{10}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces At $N=2$ and $r=1$, the reciprocal is $\sec z$. The upper row gives block exponential coefficients and the lower row gives ordinary Taylor coefficients. Both rows encode the same five terms.}}{13}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The two exact parameter polynomials from the worked calculation. Each plotted curve evaluates its displayed formula. The points at $r=1$ recover the first two positive-index secant coefficients.}}{17}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The exact order-four model. The first three zeros on each of four rays are mapped by $w=z^4$ to three positive reduced zeros. The reduced axis uses a logarithmic scale. This complete order-four example does not classify the finite exceptional set at other orders.}}{25}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Order-two moment sums over the first forty reduced zeros $\mitlambda _k=\mitpi ^2(k+1/2)^2$. The curves are finite numerical partial sums. The dashed limits $1/2$ and $1/6$ follow from the exact logarithmic coefficients and the product identity.}}{34}{figure.caption.6}%
